From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 07:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] bootmgr: Start a Boot Loader Specification entry from main
 storage

With the entries listed by m1n1 itself, the host still loaded the chosen
one block by block over the proxy and drove kboot step by step. Let m1n1
start it: P_BOOT_START takes the entry id, the board's DTB file name (or
0) and flags (0 for now), checks the entries again and refuses an entry
that isn't bootable. It loads the kernel (a 2 MiB aligned Image with room
for its image_size, or gzip, uncompressed behind the heap as payloads
are), the initrds concatenated in entry order and the devicetree, and
only then shuts storage down, so a failure before that leaves storage up
for another entry. The rest follows the payload boot: bootargs from the
entry's options, cpufreq, secondary CPUs, mitigations, the DT fill-ins,
kboot_boot. The reply goes out before the jump, as with P_KBOOT_BOOT.
On failure m1n1 stays in the proxy with the reason on its console.

The arguments are copied before anything is loaded, and the heap limit a
host may have set is lifted: the kernel slot is larger than the room
hosts usually leave m1n1, and an allocation past the limit panics.
heapblock_set_limit(NULL) now says so instead of printing a negative
size.

Assisted-by: LLM
